From 6d1ea4cbe2e09187e37e83f16eb7237b0c9ede89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LukeOkk <85941941+LukeOkk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 02:07:50 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] runtime: stop casting between vreg values and object
 pointers (SteamARM, HYPOTHESIS)

HYPOTHESIS, UNTESTED: not compiled, not run.

A dex register, an argument slot and the low half of a compiled frame's
register hold a compressed reference. These places turned one into an
object pointer with a plain cast (zero extension, which drops the heap
reference base) or back with a checked 32-bit cast (which refuses an
address above 4 GiB in debug builds):

- stack.cc: GetThisObject() and GetVReg()'s read barrier, whose
  DCHECK_LT(ptr_out, UINT32_MAX) is a below-4 GiB check;
- quick_exception_handler.cc: deoptimization's reference vregs;
- monitor.cc: the lock owner reported for a compiled frame;
- interpreter_common.cc, unstarted_runtime.cc (9 sites),
  string_builder_append.cc, dex2oat.cc: interpreter argument arrays.

They now use ReferenceFromVRegValue()/VRegValueFromReference().

Heap::IsBootImageAddress() subtracted a 32-bit boot image start from a
64-bit pointer; it decompresses the start first.

App images are not loaded while the base is non-zero: their relocation
(ImageSpace::Loader::RelocateInPlace) compares full addresses with 32-bit
range bounds and is not ported. The app keeps running from its
oat/vdex/dex files.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
